A law will enter into force in Finland on Tuesday that will restrict the procurement of substances suitable for the manufacture of explosives.
In the future, private individuals will have to seek permission from the National Police Board to buy or possess substances or mixtures that could be used as precursors of explosives.
Such substances include hydrogen peroxide, nitromethane, nitric acid, potassium chlorate, potassium per chlorate, sodium chlorate, sodium chlorate and sodium per chloretate, and mixtures containing them if prescribed concentration levels are exceeded.
Hydrogen peroxide, for example, in cleaning and detergents do not usually exceed limit values.
Nitromethane is used, for example, in accelerating racing.
Precursors acquired prior to the entry into force may be retained and used until 2 March 2016.
