ovarian cancer is more likely to die in sparsely populated areas due to late diagnosis and curtailed access to treatment
Angelina Jolie and her brother, James, have posted a video as a tribute to their mother who died of ovarian cancer in 2007.
Women living in rural Australia are at a higher risk of dying from ovarian cancer than those living in cities.
Investigators underwent more than 1,1100 Australians diagnosed with ovarian carcinoma in 2005, finding that only 35 per cent of women, more than five years after being diagnosed.
Chief Researcher Susan Jordan QIMR from the Berghofer Medical Research Research Institute confirms that women in sparsely populated and distant areas were 20 percent more likely to die during a study than women living in urban areas.
RESEARCTURER: New drugs may slow lung and ovarian cancer
Investigators underwent a seven-leautral period of women's health history.
According to doctor Susan Jordan, a woman’s age at the time of diagnosis, type of ovarian cancer, previous diseases and socio-economic status also contributed to the chance of coping.
Older women and when the cancer was more widely diagnosed, the likelihood of survival was lowest.
Feminised women in sparsely populated and proficated areas were 20 percent more likely to die during a study than women living in urban areas.
The study didn't try to find out why there's a lower likelihood of surviving ovarian cancer for women living outside of cities, but Jordan sees delayed diagnosis and trick-to-obsessed access to treatment as possible causes.
"Special practitioners in gynecological oncology treat this disease best, and their admissions are mostly located in large cities," Jordan says.
Inflammatory treatments are becoming more frequent and the importance of distance is reduced, the researcher also proposes to increase the mobility of physicians so that specialists can treat patients closer to their homes, as well as programmes to support patients being treated far from home.
According to a study by Dr. Jordan, women with ovarian carcinoma have a long-term coping rate, regardless of their geographical location, undershooting that better care is needed and better cancer prevention programs.
