Four Tips for Better Underwater Images and Videos
If you'd like to take photos or video underwater, you'll have a variety of device options to choose from.
The most favorable option is a water-resistant point-and-shoot camera, such as the Nikon Coolpix AW120 or an action camera such as the GoPro Hero3+ Silver Edition, both of which cost about buck . . .300.
Personally, I took these photos at a family celebration using a number of different cameras, each of which cost less than buck €350.
Irrespective of the device, there are a few rules to follow.
Always check your device twice.
Even if you've got a water-treasonable camera, make sure the camera's battery and other parts are sealed in a water-treabling manner.
In addition, set your camera shooting settings to match images or video.
There's a setting in some camera and video camera combinations to optimize imaging conditions underwater.
In addition, before you sleaze in the water, make sure how deep your device can take.
Some blemishes can withstand a depth of just 5 feet, others with depths of up to 50 to 60 feet.
Check out both regular and waterproof models in our purchasing guide and digital camera reviews.
Take a number of pictures – many of them fail in some way.
Compressed cameras have an LCD display that allows you to complicate the image, while action cameras often don't have this feature.
Even if you've got an LCD, it's hard to see it underwater, and you can either make an image fit or fail.
Take a number of pictures.
In addition, if your camera allows you to forhate, which means taking multiple images at once with different settings, take advantage of this feature.
Just stay close to the surface.
The light decreases significantly deeper.
If possible, stay close to the surface when shooting in a swimming pool, a lake or a sea.
This will also give you more colors for your photos; the deeper you go, the fewer colors you will see.
Just try to get close to the object being photographed.
This is an important piece of advice when filming on the ground, but even more important underwater because light conditions are scarce.
It's very important to have an action camera: these devices often have a fixed lens with a wide angle of view, i.e. the spectator needs to be close to the target to fill the object up to the frames of the entire image.
