China's leaders say Sunday's decision was a democratic breakthrough because it would allow Hong Kong to vote in direct elections, but the decision also makes it clear that China wants to strictly control the electoral process through an appointment committee that will be managed from Beijing.
According to a new clause, only candidates who "loose the country and love Hong Kong" will be accepted.
In the pre-determined summer, Hong Kong hosted some of the region's largest and highest-attulation-protching protests for years.
The pro-democracy pro-democracy campaign was largely backed by the Occupy Central With Love and Peace, whose organisers have threatened to block the business world block if Beijing did not grant Hong Kong a genuine general right to vote.
On a Sunday night, just hours after the decision was published, hundreds of Occupy Central adherents gathered in rain in front of Hong Kong's main administrative buildings.
At the rally, movement organizers indicated the movement would move into a new era of civil disobedience and they would incur protest strikes in the coming weeks.
However, they apparently did not give the details, fearing it would lead to problems with public authorities.
In an online bulletin, organizers said the movement "threatens the Occupy Central stunt as a last resort that will only be introduced if there's no hope of a dialogue and there is no other way."
We are very sorry to announce that today the last possibility of a dialogue has dawned and the overthreach of the city centre will take place.
Hong Kong authorities have been preparing for a Beijing announcement for days, and on Sunday security measures were tight near the government's main buildings, including police and barricades at the scene.
Unhappiness in Hong Kong is plagued by the feeling that residents are slowly losing control of their cities.
A flurry of arrivals has caused competition for products and services.
There is also a fear that Hong Kong's values, such as democracy and freedom of expression, are disappearing beneath Beijing's growing pressure.
Some have criticized the Occupy Central because its demonstrations threaten Hong Kong's lifeblood – the business world.
"The test they're talking about can lead to major financial losses, depending on how many people take part in it and how long it lasts," says Legislature Regina Ip, who has long criticized the movement.
We don't want you to get out of concern that Hong Kong is skirmishing our grip.
Such an image would be bad for investing.
Stories have also been put forward in Chinese state media stating that pro-democracy activists in Hong Kong are henchers of Western powers that incite a revolution."
