Genetically Deviations Are Often Diagnosed
A British woman feared death for years due to a misdiagnosis.
Karin Rodgers thought most of his adolescence that he only had a few years left to live because he was diagnosed with another disease.
He actually suffered from Charcot-Marie-Tooth (CMT). That means a range of hereditary diseases that damage nerves outside the brain and spine.
According to Charity CMT, misdiagnosis is a common problem in CMT cases because so little is known.
It is estimated that about 23,000 people in the United Kingdom are suffering from CMT disease, which causes symptoms in motor skills such as muscle weakness, tricky stratching and toe-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-toe
The disease can also cause numbness or pain, and is incurable and adhesive, which means the symptoms get worse over a period of time.
When Rodgers was 13 years old, he thought he had Friedreich's ataxia (FA), which has a really poor survival rate.
Rodgers believed he'd get into a wheelchair before turning 18 and dying before reaching the age of 30.
The 51-year-old mother-of-twiss at odds says, "As a child, I didn't know I couldn't do the same things as others.
I fell on a daily basis and it took me longer than everyone else.
I was never able to skate or skateboard with other kids, and I was tetruded at school because I was walking and running in a different way than everyone else.
When Rodgers was 13 years old, he underwent several Achilles tendon surgery to straighten his feet. After survibbing his doctor left the room, he quickly glanced at his research papers saying he was suffering from FA disease.
"I felt guilty because I was so curious, so I didn't tell anyone, but when I came home I'm intensified information about the disease at the library and wrote to the FA Association," he tells me.
By the time I got information from them, I was shocked and panicked.
I thought I'd get into a wheelchair by the age of 18 and die before I turned 25, and in between I'd slowly lose my entire ability to function.
I've been going through this by myself and planning my own funeral.
Rodgers says that by the age of 17, he stated that his walking had not worsened as much as he thought he had and asked his doctor about this.
The doctor just hugged me and said, 'Dan't honey, I don't think you've got this illness because otherwise you're already in a wheelchair.'
I think you're suffering from a far less dangerous illness.
According to some genetic tests he was diagnosed with CMT disease.
"When it was explained to me what CMT disease is, I thought I had postponed," he tells me.
The Charity CMT UK organizes a CMT month to draw attention to illness.
